      "text": "To determine the largest time value \\( t \\) at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we start with the given temperature function:\n\n\\[\nT(t) = -t^2 + 12t + 50\n\\]\n\nWe need to solve for \\( t \\) when \\( T(t) = 77 \\). This means setting up the equation:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nNext, we move all terms to one side of the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation by combining like terms:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo make it easier to handle, we can multiply the entire equation by -1 to simplify the standard form of a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nt^2 - 12t + 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis quadratic equation has two solutions. We use the quadratic formula \\( t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\):\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\nSimplify inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nThe largest \\( t \\) value is the one less than or equal to 9 since \\( t = 9 \\) is larger and represents the time when the temperature could be less than 77 degrees at some intermediate hour. Thus, the largest possible \\( t \\) value when the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
